    {
      "id": "kQLKUtnAV_CIVpgdVbca6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -817.7850578236042,
      "y": 1717.3715882554607,
      "width": 851.2242126464844,
      "height": 990,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 70,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 682437268,
      "version": 368,
      "versionNonce": 173644720,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1780940829416,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Pod Networking (the foundation) : \nEvery pod gets its own IP address, and all containers within a pod share that\nnetwork namespace. Kubernetes assumes a flat network where every pod can\ncommunicate with every other pod — regardless of which node it's on —\nwithout NAT. Pod IPs are ephemeral though; they change when a pod\nrestarts. Harness\n\n2. CNI Plugins (the plumbing):  \nWhen Kubernetes creates a pod, it invokes CNI callbacks which result in the\nCNI provider setting up IP addresses for the pod and connecting the \npod network with the host network. Popular choices are Calico, Cilium (eBPF-\nbased), and Flannel. Both Flannel and Canal use VXLAN as the backend,\nmeaning pod traffic gets encapsulated in UDP packets and routed between\nnodes. SaarasComputingForGeeks\n\n3. Services (stable virtual IPs):\nTo load-balance to service instances across pods, a ClusterIP is wired up \nso traffic sent to it can reach all pods running the service, regardless of which\nphysical node they're on. This is realized by kube-proxy using iptables, IPVS,\nor eBPF. Saaras\n\n4. CoreDNS: \nKubernetes runs CoreDNS that provides DNS resolution for services and pods,\nso you can reach a service at svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local instead of a\nraw IP. Hedgehog\n\n5. Ingress (HTTP/S routing in):\nAn Ingress resource defines HTTP(S) routing rules, letting you route external\ntraffic to different services based on hostnames or URL paths. An Ingress\nresource does nothing on its own — you need an Ingress Controller (a\nspecialized pod) that watches Ingress definitions and configures a proxy.\nHarness\n\n6. Network Policies (the firewall): \nBy default, Kubernetes allows all pods to communicate with each other.\nNetwork Policies provide firewall rules to control pod-to-pod traffic",
      "fontSize": 22,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Pod Networking (the foundation) : \nEvery pod gets its own IP address, and all containers within a pod share that network namespace. Kubernetes assumes a flat network where every pod can communicate with every other pod — regardless of which node it's on — without NAT. Pod IPs are ephemeral though; they change when a pod restarts. Harness\n\n2. CNI Plugins (the plumbing):  \nWhen Kubernetes creates a pod, it invokes CNI callbacks which result in the CNI provider setting up IP addresses for the pod and connecting the \npod network with the host network. Popular choices are Calico, Cilium (eBPF-based), and Flannel. Both Flannel and Canal use VXLAN as the backend, meaning pod traffic gets encapsulated in UDP packets and routed between nodes. SaarasComputingForGeeks\n\n3. Services (stable virtual IPs):\nTo load-balance to service instances across pods, a ClusterIP is wired up \nso traffic sent to it can reach all pods running the service, regardless of which physical node they're on. This is realized by kube-proxy using iptables, IPVS, or eBPF. Saaras\n\n4. CoreDNS: \nKubernetes runs CoreDNS that provides DNS resolution for services and pods, so you can reach a service at svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local instead of a raw IP. Hedgehog\n\n5. Ingress (HTTP/S routing in):\nAn Ingress resource defines HTTP(S) routing rules, letting you route external traffic to different services based on hostnames or URL paths. An Ingress resource does nothing on its own — you need an Ingress Controller (a specialized pod) that watches Ingress definitions and configures a proxy. Harness\n\n6. Network Policies (the firewall): \nBy default, Kubernetes allows all pods to communicate with each other. Network Policies provide firewall rules to control pod-to-pod traffic",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
